Most of them *do* have heads on them.

> So, on the Euro coins (or any coin without a head on it) how do you tell
> heads from tails? Is there something obvious I don't know after being
> isolated from nonamerican coins (except the devious Canadian dime)?

In general, there is one side with some sort of picture on it and one
side
with a number. In Dutch, head and tails is 'kop' and 'munt', which
literally
means 'head' and 'coin'; the 'munt'-side is the side with the number
on it.
That's how I remember it... 
Zorkmids are a bit more complicated. They have Castle Egreth on one
side, and the face of Belwit the Flat on the other, where it also says
'1 zorkmid'. That means that the 'kop'-side is the 'munt'-side.
However, the 'head'-side is clearly headed.
See http://quendor.robinlionheart.com/zorkmid/ for more information.
Including pictures.
